# `assert property (s_eventually <named property>);` used to be lowered SILENTLY
# WRONG. The six `TOK_EVENTUALLY expr` productions took a bare expression and
# consulted nothing, so a name that WAS a declared property became an implicitly
# declared, undriven wire.
#
# Measured on the build before this fix: the emitted design for the module below
# was a single `$check' cell with `FLAVOR "live"' whose `A' input was an undriven
# `\p_req_ack', and `write_rtlil' output was identical -- apart from `\src' line
# numbers -- to the same file with the `property ... endproperty' block DELETED.
# The declaration contributed nothing and the assertion could never fail.
#
# Refused rather than resolved, for the same reason the procedural spelling is:
# a named property is lowered into an `always' block carrying its own clocking
# event, which does not compose with a liveness operator.
logger -expect error "SVA property .p_req_ack. cannot be used as the operand of .eventually" 1
read_verilog -formal -sv <<EOT
module top (input clk, input req);
	wire ack = req;
	property p_req_ack;
		@(posedge clk) req |-> ack;
	endproperty
	assert property (s_eventually p_req_ack);
endmodule
EOT
